# Reuse types

Do not invent a new type when an existing domain type (or a `Pick` / intersection of one) already describes the data.

- Prefer the shared or package **domain** type as the source of truth.
- Extend with `& { … }` or narrow with `Pick` / `Omit` instead of copying fields into a sibling interface.
- UI props, tooltips, and layer payloads should reference domain types — not redefine `name`, `id`, totals, etc.
- Inline `{ id: string; name: string }` is fine for a one-off parameter; do not export a new named type for it unless reused in several places.
- Before adding a type, search for an existing one with the same fields or meaning.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — parallel shapes for the same neighborhood spending
export type NeighborhoodSpending = {
  neighborhoodId: string;
  contractCount: number;
  totalAwardedEur: number;
};
export type NeighborhoodLabel = {
  neighborhoodId: string;
  name: string;
  contractCount: number;
  totalAwardedEur: number;
  position: [number, number];
};
export type NeighborhoodTooltipPayload = {
  neighborhoodId: string;
  name: string;
  contractCount: number;
  totalAwardedEur: number;
};

// ✅ GOOD — one domain type; derive UI shapes
export type NeighborhoodSpending = {
  neighborhoodId: string;
  contractCount: number;
  totalAwardedEur: number;
};
export type NeighborhoodSpendingFeatureProperties = NeighborhoodSpending & {
  name: string;
  labelLongitude: number;
  labelLatitude: number;
};
export type NeighborhoodSpendingTooltipState = Pick<
  NeighborhoodSpendingFeatureProperties,
  'name' | 'contractCount' | 'totalAwardedEur'
> & { x: number; y: number };
```
